<%# The bar a held confirmation wears in place of the archive's: the way back
    to the pen, and the two endings a hold has.

    Done is a button the reader presses, and it has to stay one. The confirm
    link belongs to the sender and is in the frame below, whose sandbox grants
    allow-popups precisely so it works — but that frame has an opaque origin
    and no scripts, so nothing here can see the click, the navigation, or the
    page it lands on. Anything that dismissed a hold on its own would be
    guessing: a reader who opened the email and read it is indistinguishable
    from one who confirmed. A wrong guess loses the subscription silently,
    which is the failure this whole feature exists to prevent. Do not automate
    it. Nothing that arrives later — a first issue from the same sender, a
    timeout, a scroll depth — is evidence either.

    Both endings are POSTs, so both are button_to. Neither is hidden below
    720px the way the reader's second action is: a phone is where mail gets
    read, and a misfire the reader cannot release from one stays in the pen. %>
<header class="topbar topbar--pen">
  <%= link_to t("newsletters.original.pen.back"),
        subscriptions_path,
        class: "topbar__back" %>

  <div class="topbar__actions">
    <%= button_to t("newsletters.original.pen.release"),
          newsletter_release_path(newsletter),
          class: "topbar__action",
          form_class: "topbar__form" %>
    <%= button_to t("newsletters.original.pen.done"),
          newsletter_dismissal_path(newsletter),
          class: "topbar__action topbar__action--done",
          form_class: "topbar__form" %>
  </div>
</header>
